# PXE Boot Lab: DHCP and TFTP Server Configuration

> **Lesson focus:** Understanding how the DHCP server tells a PXE client where to find its network boot file.

## 1. DHCP Server Configuration

The DHCP server has already been configured for the PXE boot lab.

In addition to providing network configuration to the client, the DHCP configuration includes:

- The **IP address of the TFTP server**.
- The **PXE boot filename** that the client should request.

## 2. DHCP and TFTP on the Same Server

For this lab, the **DHCP server and TFTP server are hosted on the same machine**.

Therefore, the TFTP server IP address supplied in the DHCP configuration is the same IP address as the DHCP server.

> **Note:** The lecture does not provide the actual server IP address or PXE boot filename.

## 3. The `next-server` Setting

The `next-server` setting identifies the IP address of the TFTP server from which the PXE client should retrieve its boot file.

Conceptually:

```text
PXE Client
    |
    | Requests network boot information
    v
DHCP Server
    |
    | Provides TFTP server IP (next-server)
    | and PXE boot filename
    v
PXE Client
    |
    | Requests the specified boot file
    v
TFTP Server
```

In this lab, the DHCP and TFTP server roles run on the same host.

## 4. PXE Boot Filename

The DHCP configuration also specifies the **name of the PXE boot file**.

The filename supplied to the client must match the file made available by the TFTP server.

## 5. Place the Boot File in the TFTP Root

The PXE boot file referenced by DHCP must be placed in the **root directory served by TFTP**.

```text
DHCP configuration
    |
    +-- next-server: TFTP server IP address
    |
    +-- filename: PXE boot filename
                         |
                         v
                 TFTP server root
                         |
                         +-- Matching PXE boot file
```

The exact TFTP root directory and boot filename are not specified in the lecture; these will depend on the TFTP server configuration.

## 6. Next Lab Session

The next lecture will cover **configuring the TFTP server** so it can serve the PXE boot file specified in DHCP.

## Key Takeaways

- The DHCP server has already been configured.
- DHCP identifies the TFTP server using `next-server`.
- DHCP also provides the PXE boot filename.
- In this lab, DHCP and TFTP run on the same machine and use the same server IP address.
- The named boot file must exist under the TFTP server's configured root directory.
- TFTP server setup is the next step in the PXE boot lab.
